        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 2.6 The Liquidity Premium Puzzle",
            "block_bbox": [
                139,
                224,
                574,
                250
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    139.0,
                    224.0
                ],
                [
                    574.0,
                    224.0
                ],
                [
                    574.0,
                    250.0
                ],
                [
                    139.0,
                    250.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our paper also contributes to the literature on the liquidity premium puzzle. Merton (1973) hypothesized that investors should demand higher returns to hold illiquid assets to compensate for transaction costs, but he left open which market participants actually bear that cost. Constantinides (1986) formalized this as a puzzle: in an equilibrium model, the liquidity premium should be small for investors with an infinite trading horizon, yet large liquidity premia are observed in practice. Chen (2022) attributes the discrepancy to heterogeneity in investor information, arguing that better-informed investors are unfazed by transaction costs, while less-informed investors require compensation for illiquidity. Fama and French (1988) separately establish the empirical practice of decomposing security returns into permanent and transitory components, a decomposition we adapt in interpreting the temporary mispricing mechanism below.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                263,
                1083,
                479
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    263.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    263.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    479.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    479.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our proposed resolution synthesizes the information-asymmetry mechanism of Kyle (1985) and Glosten and Milgrom (1985) with this puzzle: rather than positing a counterparty who knowingly pays a risk premium to illiquid-asset holders, we show that low signed order flow depresses prices today because Kyle's  $ \\lambda $ widens when noise-trading variance is low relative to informational uncertainty, discouraging participation. Subsequent normalization of order flow and the associated narrowing of  $ \\lambda $ then drives a price recovery, generating a return differential that requires no counterparty to \"pay\" the premium in equilibrium. This relocates the liquidity premium puzzle's resolution from a risk-compensation story to an adverse-selection and price-impact story, consistent with the market microstructure tradition reviewed above.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                480,
                1083,
                669
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    480.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    480.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    669.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    669.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In sum, while the existing literature establishes (1) equilibrium models of price formation under information asymmetry, (2) empirical techniques for measuring informed trading and price discovery, and (3) the presence of equity liquidity premia, no prior work directly estimates Kyle's  $ \\lambda $ from observable order flow and tests its ability to forecast the cross-section of equity returns within a unified, microstructure-founded framework. Our contribution fills this gap.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                670,
                1083,
                791
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    670.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    670.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    791.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    791.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "## 3 The Model",
            "block_bbox": [
                140,
                825,
                351,
                853
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    140.0,
                    825.0
                ],
                [
                    351.0,
                    825.0
                ],
                [
                    351.0,
                    853.0
                ],
                [
                    140.0,
                    853.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In this section, we propose an agent-driven pricing model. Specifically, we establish that",
            "block_bbox": [
                177,
                875,
                956,
                899
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    177.0,
                    875.0
                ],
                [
                    956.0,
                    875.0
                ],
                [
                    956.0,
                    899.0
                ],
                [
                    177.0,
                    899.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1. For assets with a finite outstanding amount, short-term informed trading necessarily induces higher trading volume in the long run.",
            "block_bbox": [
                164,
                916,
                1081,
                963
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    164.0,
                    916.0
                ],
                [
                    1081.0,
                    916.0
                ],
                [
                    1081.0,
                    963.0
                ],
                [
                    164.0,
                    963.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "2. In addition, in the long run, higher volume translates into higher demand for the asset and results in higher prices.",
            "block_bbox": [
                163,
                979,
                1082,
                1027
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
